From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 16:58:22 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 158/209] feat: three real colour wheels for the CineCam Grade,
 in a dockable panel (S10 item 5)

Tools -> Cinematic Camera -> CineCam Grade. The three Primaries wheels are hue/saturation discs -
offset direction is the hue, distance is the strength, the centre is exactly neutral - with the
master beside each disc, double-click or right-click to reset, and the numbers next to them.
Contrast and pivot, saturation, both five-point curves, the ASC CDL base and the look slot are in
the same window, so the whole grade is on screen at once.

Decision: scene-notes/decisions/s10-grade-component.md, "Item 5" (alternatives A/C/D kept).
Research, written first: scene-notes/research/s10-5-colour-wheel-widget.md.

Why a panel and not a property row in the inspector. A row was traced end to end and works
(the inspector is the legacy tree, REGISTER_PROPERTY_ROW is exported from EditorCommon,
Schematyc::Range is the precedent for decorating a reflected member without changing the saved XML
or the TrackView track, and floorHeight() makes a row disc-sized), but Lift and LiftMaster are two
separate reflected members and a decorator only ever sees the one it decorates - merging them would
rename members whose names item 3a froze for TrackView. A row also has no room for three discs and
registers process-wide against a type name.

How it stays correct:

* Live. The camera calls ApplyGrade() on every ENTITY_EVENT_UPDATE and pulls this component's
  structs then, so writing the members is enough for the viewport; the panel still sends
  ENTITY_EVENT_COMPONENT_PROPERTY_CHANGED to save that frame and to re-run the component's
  ReportStatus().
* Undo. IUndoManager::Begin() + CBaseObject::StoreUndo on the first change of a gesture, Accept on
  the release - the same pair CObjectPropertyWidget opens for every inspector edit, and
  CUndoBaseObject serialises the whole object, components included. So one drag is one Ctrl+Z, and
  a click that changes nothing leaves no step at all.
* Saved. The level writes the live components; for a Schematyc entity the panel additionally
  repeats EntityObject.cpp:925-928 (SetOverridePolicy + CClassProperties::Read), because a write
  from outside a serialization pass misses that snapshot - the same trap items 3a and 4b document.
* Consistent. The panel is a view: every editor idle it re-reads the component and repaints if the
  numbers moved, so an inspector edit, an undo or an animated grade show up in it by themselves.
  It owns the values only while something in it is being dragged.

How it reaches the component: by reflection, never by linking it. CCineGradeComponent's members are
private and its methods are unexported, so CineGradeBinding resolves each value through
GetClassDesc() -> CClassMemberDesc::GetOffsetPointer() and checks every member's type GUID; a
member that is missing or of an unexpected type is refused by name in the console and the panel
stays unbound rather than writing to an offset it is not sure about. Consequence, accepted: Export
Preset and Re-apply Preset stay on the component in the inspector - they are ActionButtons the
property tree calls, and reaching them would need a new exported interface.

Binding follows the user's rule: exactly one selected entity carrying a CineCam Grade is editable
and named in the header; anything else is read-only and greyed with the reason, keeping the last
numbers on screen.

TrackView seam, built but not used: every write goes through IGradeEditTarget (IsValid / Read /
BeginEdit / Write / EndEdit). Today one implementation - the selected entity's component. A future
TrackView-aware target answers the same four calls by keying the tracks at the play head; no widget
would change. No TrackView work in this commit.

New files, all in Code/Sandbox/Plugins/CinematicCameraEditor (one DLL, EditorCommon only - no
Sandbox, no EditorQt, nothing to deploy but CinematicCameraEditor.dll):
CineGradeBinding.h/.cpp (values, the target interface, reflection, undo),
CineGradeWidgets.h/.cpp (the disc, the wheel control, the labelled float row),
CineGradePanel.h/.cpp (the pane, selection binding, the idle poll).
Renderer, engine plugin and shaders untouched. README and docs/SceneReferredLook.md say where the
wheels are.

